
TASK 4 – Use Macros Safely (4 Marks)
1. Brief explanation
Macros are defined using #define and processed by the C preprocessor before compilation. They help reuse small expressions, but unsafe macros can produce incorrect results because the preprocessor performs text substitution rather than normal function evaluation.

Examples of useful macros include converting rupees to paise and finding the larger of two numbers.

CODE:


#include <stdio.h>

#define RUPEES_TO_PAISE(r) ((r) * 100)
#define SQUARE(x) ((x) * (x))
#define MAX(a, b) ((a) > (b) ? (a) : (b))

// Static inline alternatives
static inline int square(int x)
{
    return x * x;
}

static inline int max_int(int a, int b)
{
    return (a > b) ? a : b;
}

int main(void)
{
    int a, b;

    printf("Enter two integers: ");

    if (scanf("%d %d", &a, &b) != 2)
    {
        printf("Invalid input!\n");
        return 1;
    }

    printf("Rupees to paise: %d\n", RUPEES_TO_PAISE(a));
    printf("Square of (a + 1): %d\n", SQUARE(a + 1));
    printf("Maximum using macro: %d\n", MAX(a, b));
    printf("Square using inline function: %d\n", square(a));
    printf("Maximum using inline function: %d\n", max_int(a, b));

    return 0;
}




SAMPLE INPUT:

5 8

OUTPUT:

Enter two integers: 5 8
Rupees to paise: 500
Square of (a + 1): 36
Maximum using macro: 8
Square using inline function: 25
Maximum using inline function: 8







